From: Kamil Bienkiewicz <perceivalpercy@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] net: ethernet: qualcomm: ppe: program MY_MAC and the ingress
 L3 interface

With the APPE register map fixed, the driver programs host, flow,
nexthop and public-IP entries that hardware accepts -- and then drops
every packet of an offloaded flow instead of forwarding it. Measured on
an IPQ5332 (GL.iNet BE9300): the WAN port's TX counter moved 13 packets
during a 15s transfer while its RX counter moved 403 (the peer's
retransmissions), and the same transfer completes with
flow_offloading_hw=0.

Hardware only routes a frame whose destination MAC it recognises as its
own, on an ingress interface with unicast routing enabled. Neither was
configured. Read back live from the device:

  MY_MAC_TBL (0x200000)     all 8 entries zero
  IN_L3_IF_TBL MAC_BITMAP   0
  IN_L3_IF_TBL IPV4_UC_ROUTE_EN  0
  IN_L3_IF_TBL MRU/MTU      1500/1500 (already set)

so no frame could be routed regardless of what the flow table said. The
vendor driver programs all of it (ppe_drv_l3_if.c: MY_MAC add/release
plus the ingress MTU/MRU), while this driver only ever programmed the
egress L3 interface entry.

Add the two missing primitives -- a MY_MAC table entry, and the port to
ingress-L3-interface binding in L3_VP_PORT_TBL (read-modify-write, as
the rest of that entry is unrelated source-guard configuration) -- and
program them per EDMA port alongside the existing egress entry, with
the MRU/MTU and routing enable the ingress entry needs. The ingress
entry now also follows an MTU change, which hardware enforces.

This is scoped to the ports this driver owns. It does not attempt the
VSI/bridge ingress path, which resolves its L3 interface through
L3_VSI instead.

Enabling ingress routing also makes the flow table's ingress qualifier
load-bearing, so set it. A flow entry matches on its 5-tuple alone
unless it names an ingress L3 interface, which would let an offloaded
flow be forwarded no matter which port a matching frame arrived on,
ignoring the interface-scoped policy (firewall zones, the WAN/LAN
split) the kernel applies in software -- an on-segment host could
inject another client's 5-tuple and have it hardware-NAT'd out. The
vendor driver qualifies every flow the same way (ppe_drv_flow.c sets
src_intf_valid unconditionally). The ingress device comes from the
rule's own FLOW_DISSECTOR_KEY_META ingress_ifindex, resolved through a
VLAN upper or DSA user port to the EDMA netdevice that receives the
frame; a flow whose ingress cannot be resolved is declined rather than
offloaded unqualified. Note this board's DSA user ports share one
conduit and therefore one ingress L3 interface, so hardware can
distinguish LAN from WAN but not one LAN port from another.

The ingress entry's TTL-exceed action is set to redirect to the CPU:
hardware decrements the TTL for a routed frame, and the zeroed default
(FAL_MAC_FRWRD) would forward an expired one instead of letting the
CPU answer it.

Enabling ingress routing is safe against a lookup miss: L3_ROUTE_CTRL
and L3_ROUTE_CTRL_EXT are left at their power-on values, where every
failure action (MRU, MTU, PMTU, route mismatch, no-Ethernet) is
RDT_TO_CPU, so anything hardware cannot resolve falls back to the
software path rather than being dropped. Verified by reading both
registers on the live device (0x0dbc36db / 0x00031b23).

Not yet hardware-tested: whether this is sufficient to make an
offloaded flow actually forward.

Signed-off-by: Kamil Bienkiewicz <perceivalpercy@gmail.com>
---
--- a/drivers/net/ethernet/qualcomm/ppe/ppe_regs.h
+++ b/drivers/net/ethernet/qualcomm/ppe/ppe_regs.h
@@ -1215,6 +1215,40 @@
  * whole-word value, so need no bitfield mask.
  */
 
+/* MY_MAC table: the MAC addresses the PPE treats as its own. Hardware
+ * only routes a frame whose destination MAC matches an entry here that
+ * the ingress L3 interface selects through its MAC_BITMAP -- an
+ * unmatched frame is never routed, so a flow entry alone is not enough.
+ * Addressed directly, two words per entry, at IPE_L3_BASE_ADDR + 0x0
+ * (vendor MY_MAC_TBL, struct my_mac_tbl). The MAC is split the same
+ * way as PPE_EG_L3_IF_TBL's: low 32 bits in word 0, high 16 bits in
+ * word 1 -- confirmed against the vendor's own MY_MAC programming
+ * (adpt_hppe_ip.c), which copies both halves straight from the egress
+ * L3 interface entry it just built.
+ */
+#define PPE_MY_MAC_TBL_ADDR			0x200000
+#define PPE_MY_MAC_TBL_ENTRIES			8
+#define PPE_MY_MAC_TBL_INC			0x8
+
+/* Word 0 (MAC_DA_0) is a single whole-word value, no bitfield mask. */
+
+/* Word 1. */
+#define PPE_MY_MAC_TBL_MAC_DA_1			GENMASK(15, 0)
+#define PPE_MY_MAC_TBL_VALID			BIT(16)
+
+/* L3 virtual-port table: binds an ingress port to the L3 interface
+ * entry consulted for frames arriving on it. Only the L3 interface
+ * binding is touched here; the rest of the entry holds unrelated
+ * source-guard configuration, so it is updated read-modify-write.
+ */
+#define PPE_L3_VP_PORT_TBL_ADDR			0x204000
+#define PPE_L3_VP_PORT_TBL_ENTRIES		256
+#define PPE_L3_VP_PORT_TBL_INC			0x10
+
+/* Word 0. */
+#define PPE_L3_VP_PORT_TBL_L3_IF_VALID		BIT(0)
+#define PPE_L3_VP_PORT_TBL_L3_IF_INDEX		GENMASK(8, 1)
+
 /* Ingress L3 interface table: per-L3-interface MTU/MRU/route-enable
  * config, referenced by a flow entry's SRC_L3_IF and consulted on
  * ingress. Addressed directly, three words per entry on APPE (the
--- a/drivers/net/ethernet/qualcomm/ppe/ppe_l3_if.h
+++ b/drivers/net/ethernet/qualcomm/ppe/ppe_l3_if.h
@@ -12,6 +12,20 @@
 #include "ppe.h"
 
 /**
+ * enum ppe_l3_if_action - What hardware does with a frame that fails a check.
+ * @PPE_L3_IF_ACTION_FORWARD: Forward it anyway.
+ * @PPE_L3_IF_ACTION_DROP: Drop it.
+ * @PPE_L3_IF_ACTION_COPY_TO_CPU: Forward it and copy it to the CPU.
+ * @PPE_L3_IF_ACTION_REDIRECT_TO_CPU: Send it to the CPU instead.
+ */
+enum ppe_l3_if_action {
+	PPE_L3_IF_ACTION_FORWARD = 0,
+	PPE_L3_IF_ACTION_DROP,
+	PPE_L3_IF_ACTION_COPY_TO_CPU,
+	PPE_L3_IF_ACTION_REDIRECT_TO_CPU,
+};
+
+/**
  * struct ppe_l3_if_in_entry - PPE ingress L3 interface table entry.
  * @mru: Maximum receive unit enforced on this L3 interface, in bytes.
  * @mtu: Maximum transmit unit enforced on this L3 interface, in bytes.
@@ -60,6 +74,10 @@
 	bool pppoe_en;
 };
 
+int ppe_my_mac_entry_set(struct ppe_device *ppe_dev, u32 index,
+			  const u8 *mac_addr, bool valid);
+int ppe_l3_if_port_bind(struct ppe_device *ppe_dev, u32 port_id,
+			 u32 l3_if_index, bool valid);
 int ppe_l3_if_in_entry_set(struct ppe_device *ppe_dev, u32 index,
 			    const struct ppe_l3_if_in_entry *entry);
 int ppe_l3_if_in_entry_get(struct ppe_device *ppe_dev, u32 index,
--- a/drivers/net/ethernet/qualcomm/ppe/ppe_l3_if.c
+++ b/drivers/net/ethernet/qualcomm/ppe/ppe_l3_if.c
@@ -43,6 +43,13 @@
 	if (index >= PPE_IN_L3_IF_TBL_ENTRIES)
 		return -EINVAL;
 
+	/* FIELD_PREP() would silently truncate these to the field width. */
+	if (entry->mru > FIELD_MAX(PPE_IN_L3_IF_TBL_MRU) ||
+	    entry->mtu > FIELD_MAX(PPE_IN_L3_IF_TBL_MTU) ||
+	    entry->mru_ipv6 > FIELD_MAX(PPE_IN_L3_IF_TBL_MRU_IPV6) ||
+	    entry->mtu_ipv6 > FIELD_MAX(PPE_IN_L3_IF_TBL_MTU_IPV6))
+		return -EINVAL;
+
 	val[0] = FIELD_PREP(PPE_IN_L3_IF_TBL_MRU, entry->mru);
 	val[0] |= FIELD_PREP(PPE_IN_L3_IF_TBL_MTU, entry->mtu);
 	val[0] |= entry->ttl_dec_bypass ? PPE_IN_L3_IF_TBL_TTL_DEC_BYPASS : 0;
@@ -182,3 +189,77 @@
 
 	return 0;
 }
+
+/**
+ * ppe_my_mac_entry_set() - Write a PPE MY_MAC table entry.
+ * @ppe_dev: PPE device.
+ * @index: The entry index to write.
+ * @mac_addr: The MAC address hardware should treat as its own.
+ * @valid: Whether the entry is active. When false, @mac_addr is
+ * ignored and the entry is cleared.
+ *
+ * An ingress L3 interface selects the entries that apply to it through
+ * its MAC_BITMAP, so an entry written here only takes effect once some
+ * interface names it.
+ *
+ * Return: 0 on success, a negative error code on failure.
+ */
+int ppe_my_mac_entry_set(struct ppe_device *ppe_dev, u32 index,
+			  const u8 *mac_addr, bool valid)
+{
+	u32 base, val[2] = {};
+	u64 mac;
+	int ret;
+	int i;
+
+	if (index >= PPE_MY_MAC_TBL_ENTRIES)
+		return -EINVAL;
+
+	if (valid) {
+		mac = ether_addr_to_u64(mac_addr);
+		val[0] = mac & GENMASK(31, 0);
+		val[1] = FIELD_PREP(PPE_MY_MAC_TBL_MAC_DA_1, mac >> 32);
+		val[1] |= PPE_MY_MAC_TBL_VALID;
+	}
+
+	base = PPE_MY_MAC_TBL_ADDR + index * PPE_MY_MAC_TBL_INC;
+	for (i = 0; i < 2; i++) {
+		ret = regmap_write(ppe_dev->regmap, base + i * 4, val[i]);
+		if (ret)
+			return ret;
+	}
+
+	return 0;
+}
+
+/**
+ * ppe_l3_if_port_bind() - Bind a port to an ingress L3 interface entry.
+ * @ppe_dev: PPE device.
+ * @port_id: The port whose arriving frames the binding applies to.
+ * @l3_if_index: The ingress L3 interface table index to consult.
+ * @valid: Whether the binding is active.
+ *
+ * The rest of this table entry holds unrelated source-guard
+ * configuration, so only the two L3 interface fields are updated.
+ *
+ * Return: 0 on success, a negative error code on failure.
+ */
+int ppe_l3_if_port_bind(struct ppe_device *ppe_dev, u32 port_id,
+			 u32 l3_if_index, bool valid)
+{
+	u32 addr, val = 0;
+
+	if (port_id >= PPE_L3_VP_PORT_TBL_ENTRIES ||
+	    l3_if_index >= PPE_IN_L3_IF_TBL_ENTRIES)
+		return -EINVAL;
+
+	if (valid)
+		val = PPE_L3_VP_PORT_TBL_L3_IF_VALID |
+		      FIELD_PREP(PPE_L3_VP_PORT_TBL_L3_IF_INDEX, l3_if_index);
+
+	addr = PPE_L3_VP_PORT_TBL_ADDR + port_id * PPE_L3_VP_PORT_TBL_INC;
+
+	return regmap_update_bits(ppe_dev->regmap, addr,
+				   PPE_L3_VP_PORT_TBL_L3_IF_VALID |
+				   PPE_L3_VP_PORT_TBL_L3_IF_INDEX, val);
+}
--- a/drivers/net/ethernet/qualcomm/ppe/ppe_offload.c
+++ b/drivers/net/ethernet/qualcomm/ppe/ppe_offload.c
@@ -73,6 +73,7 @@
 #include <linux/list.h>
 #include <linux/mutex.h>
 #include <linux/netdevice.h>
+#include <net/dsa.h>
 #include <linux/rhashtable.h>
 #include <linux/slab.h>
 #include <linux/tcp.h>
@@ -92,6 +93,9 @@
 #include "ppe_pub_ip_pool.h"
 #include "ppe_regs.h"
 
+/* No EDMA port owns this L3 interface index. */
+#define PPE_OFFLOAD_L3_IF_NONE U32_MAX
+
 /* Total number of hardware-addressable flow-table slots, derived from
  * the width of the index field hardware reports a HASH-mode add's
  * result in -- used to size priv->flow_owner (see struct
@@ -234,6 +238,47 @@
 #define ppe_offload_decline(fmt, ...) \
 	({ pr_info_ratelimited("ppe_offload: declined - " fmt "\n", ##__VA_ARGS__); -EOPNOTSUPP; })
 
+/* Resolve the device a flow's packets arrive on down to the EDMA port
+ * whose ingress L3 interface hardware will match against, mirroring
+ * the redirect-target resolution done for egress. A VLAN upper or a
+ * DSA user port both sit on top of the EDMA netdevice that actually
+ * receives the frame.
+ *
+ * Note all of this board's DSA user ports share one conduit, so they
+ * share one ingress L3 interface: hardware can qualify a flow to the
+ * LAN side or the WAN side, but not to an individual LAN port.
+ *
+ * Must be called under RCU: the device is not held, and neither is
+ * anything this walks through to reach it.
+ *
+ * Return: the EDMA netdevice, or NULL if it isn't one.
+ */
+static struct net_device *ppe_offload_ingress_edma_dev(struct net_device *dev)
+{
+#if IS_ENABLED(CONFIG_NET_DSA)
+	struct dsa_port *dp;
+#endif
+
+	if (is_vlan_dev(dev))
+		dev = vlan_dev_real_dev(dev);
+
+#if IS_ENABLED(CONFIG_NET_DSA)
+	/* A DSA user port resolves to the conduit that receives its
+	 * frames; anything else (including the conduit itself) fails
+	 * this lookup and is used as-is, mirroring
+	 * mtk_flow_get_dsa_port().
+	 */
+	dp = dsa_port_from_netdev(dev);
+	if (!IS_ERR(dp))
+		dev = dsa_port_to_conduit(dp);
+#endif
+
+	if (!dev || !edma_port_is_edma_netdev(dev))
+		return NULL;
+
+	return dev;
+}
+
 static int ppe_offload_flow_replace(struct net_device *netdev,
 				     struct ppe_offload_priv *priv,
 				     struct flow_cls_offload *cls)
@@ -247,6 +292,9 @@
 	struct ppe_nexthop_entry nexthop;
 	struct edma_port_priv *port_priv;
 	struct net_device *odev = NULL;
+	struct net_device *idev;
+	struct flow_match_meta meta;
+	u32 ingress_l3_if = PPE_OFFLOAD_L3_IF_NONE;
 	__be32 match_src, match_dst;
 	__be32 new_src, new_dst;
 	__be16 match_sport, match_dport;
@@ -306,6 +354,39 @@
 		match_dport = ports.key->dst;
 	}
 
+	/* Hardware matches a flow on its 5-tuple alone unless the entry
+	 * names an ingress L3 interface, so without this an offloaded
+	 * flow would be forwarded no matter which port a matching frame
+	 * arrived on -- ignoring the interface-scoped policy (firewall
+	 * zones, WAN/LAN split) the kernel applies in software. The
+	 * vendor driver qualifies every flow the same way.
+	 */
+	if (!flow_rule_match_key(rule, FLOW_DISSECTOR_KEY_META))
+		return ppe_offload_decline("no ingress interface in rule");
+
+	flow_rule_match_meta(rule, &meta);
+	if (!meta.key->ingress_ifindex || meta.mask->ingress_ifindex != ~0u)
+		return ppe_offload_decline("ingress ifindex %d not an exact match",
+					    meta.key->ingress_ifindex);
+
+	/* The device is only used to resolve the index it maps to, so
+	 * take it under RCU rather than holding a reference.
+	 */
+	rcu_read_lock();
+	idev = dev_get_by_index_rcu(dev_net(netdev), meta.key->ingress_ifindex);
+	if (idev)
+		idev = ppe_offload_ingress_edma_dev(idev);
+	if (idev)
+		ingress_l3_if = ((struct edma_port_priv *)netdev_priv(idev))->l3_if_index;
+	rcu_read_unlock();
+
+	if (ingress_l3_if == PPE_OFFLOAD_L3_IF_NONE)
+		return ppe_offload_decline("ingress ifindex %d not on an edma netdev",
+					    meta.key->ingress_ifindex);
+
+	if (ingress_l3_if > FIELD_MAX(PPE_FLOW_TBL_SRC_L3_IF))
+		return ppe_offload_decline("ingress l3_if %u out of range", ingress_l3_if);
+
 	new_src = match_src;
 	new_dst = match_dst;
 	l4.source = match_sport;
@@ -485,6 +566,8 @@
 	flow_ipv4.match_ip = match_dst;
 	flow_ipv4.match_sport = be16_to_cpu(match_sport);
 	flow_ipv4.match_dport = be16_to_cpu(match_dport);
+	flow_ipv4.src_l3_if_valid = true;
+	flow_ipv4.src_l3_if = ingress_l3_if;
 	flow_ipv4.pmtu = pmtu;
 	flow_ipv4.pmtu_check_l3 = true;
 	ppe_flow_ipv4_snat_entry_encode(&flow_ipv4, flow_words);
--- a/drivers/net/ethernet/qualcomm/ppe/edma_port.h
+++ b/drivers/net/ethernet/qualcomm/ppe/edma_port.h
@@ -84,10 +84,12 @@
  * @pcpu_stats: Per CPU netdev statistics
